firedamp-api — Cloudflare Worker
================================

Proxies plume-analysis requests to OpenRouter (Gemini 2.5 Flash via JSON
schema-mode structured outputs), holding the API key as a Wrangler
secret so it never ships to the browser. Caches every response in D1
for free repeat reads and as a queryable dataset.


Endpoints
---------

POST /api/analyse
    Body: { plumeId, prompt, image, lat, lon, dt, rate, src,
            place, windSpeed, windDirFrom, force? }
    Streams Server-Sent Events compatible with OpenRouter's chat-
    completion shape. The model is constrained to a JSON schema:
        { source_label, source_kind, attributed_id, paragraph }
    Response header X-Firedamp-Cache is "hit" when served from D1 and
    "miss" when freshly generated. ?force=1 / { force: true } skips
    the cache and writes a new row, leaving the old one for comparison.

GET /api/analysis/<plumeId>
    Lightweight peek used by the browser before deciding to run the
    full pipeline. Returns the latest cached row (response + parsed
    columns + wind + place) so a returning user gets an instant render
    and we skip Overpass, Nominatim, Open-Meteo, image capture, and
    OpenRouter entirely. 404 means "no cached analysis yet".

GET /api/analyses?limit=&since=
    Recent analyses as JSON. Origin-checked.

GET /healthz
    Liveness check.


D1 schema
---------
See schema.sql. One row per (plume_id, model, prompt_sha) — when the
prompt template changes, old rows are kept and a new prompt produces a
new row, so we get full history. Indexes on plume_id+model+created_at,
attributed_id, lat+lon, plume_date, created_at.

Stored fields include the raw JSON response plus parsed convenience
columns: source_label, source_kind, attributed_id, paragraph,
place_name, wind_speed, wind_dir_from.


First-time setup
----------------

    cd worker

    # 1. Create the D1 database. Copy the printed database_id into
    #    wrangler.toml under [[d1_databases]].
    npx wrangler d1 create firedamp-analyses

    # 2. Apply the schema.
    npx wrangler d1 execute firedamp-analyses --file schema.sql --remote

    # 3. Set the OpenRouter key.
    npx wrangler secret put OPENROUTER_KEY

    # 4. Deploy.
    npx wrangler deploy

The deployed Worker URL ends up at firedamp-api.<account>.workers.dev.
Paste it into web/index.html as <meta name="firedamp-api" content="…">.


Common ops
----------
The top-level Makefile wraps these:

    make worker-dev      wrangler dev on :8787 (browser: ?api=local)
    make worker-deploy   wrangler deploy
    make worker-schema   apply schema.sql to remote D1
    make worker-tail     tail Worker logs


Operational notes
-----------------

- ALLOWED_ORIGINS in wrangler.toml gates the browser callers. Referer
  and Origin can be spoofed by a determined caller, so the hard guard
  is the OpenRouter key's own credit limit (set one in the OpenRouter
  dashboard, especially if exposing this more widely).

- Cache key is (plume_id, model, sha256(prompt)). Editing the prompt
  template doesn't invalidate anything — old rows persist, the new
  prompt just produces a new row keyed by the new sha. The latest
  matching row wins on read.

- The peek endpoint returns the most recent row for a plume_id,
  regardless of prompt_sha. A returning user may see a response
  generated from an older prompt template until they regenerate.

- D1 free tier: 5 GB, 5M reads/day, 100k writes/day — far above what
  firedamp is going to use.
